1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,722
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces First-order Repulsive Deep
Ensembles, or FoRDE,</font>

2
00:00:04,802 --> 00:00:07,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new way to train neural network ensembles.</font>

3
00:00:07,546 --> 00:00:11,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">Deep ensembles work well because their members
learn diverse functions,</font>

4
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:17,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">but existing methods that repel members in weight
space or function space have struggled</font>

5
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:19,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">to improve on plain deep ensembles.</font>

6
00:00:19,643 --> 00:00:25,362
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key idea here is to instead repel ensemble
members in the space of their input gradients.</font>

7
00:00:25,442 --> 00:00:32,346
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because input gradients uniquely characterize a
function up to translation and are far smaller than the weights,</font>

8
00:00:32,426 --> 00:00:39,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">this guarantees members become functionally
different and encourages each network to learn distinct features.</font>

9
00:00:39,222 --> 00:00:45,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is an ensemble that is markedly more
robust and better calibrated under input corruptions.</font>

10
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:52,668
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ensembles of neural networks are powerful because
different members capture different explanations of the data.</font>

11
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:58,036
<font color="#FFFFFF">Particle-based variational inference tries to make
this diversity explicit by adding a</font>

12
00:00:58,116 --> 00:01:00,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">repulsion term that pushes members apart.</font>

13
00:01:00,675 --> 00:01:03,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">But where you apply that repulsion matters.</font>

14
00:01:03,359 --> 00:01:05,651
<font color="#FFFFFF">Repelling in weight space is wasteful,</font>

15
00:01:05,731 --> 00:01:12,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">because neural networks are heavily
over-parameterized and many different weights encode the same function.</font>

16
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:15,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">Repelling directly in function space sounds appealing,</font>

17
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:20,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it requires comparing entire functions, which
is computationally hard,</font>

18
00:01:20,399 --> 00:01:23,877
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the shortcuts used in prior work led to underfitting.</font>

19
00:01:23,957 --> 00:01:30,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">So neither weight-space nor function-space
repulsion had delivered meaningful gains over standard deep ensembles.</font>

20
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:34,785
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors take a third view of a neural network.</font>

21
00:01:34,865 --> 00:01:38,341
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond its weights and its function values, a model
can be represented,</font>

22
00:01:38,421 --> 00:01:41,896
<font color="#FFFFFF">up to a translation, by its first-order input
gradients, that is,</font>

23
00:01:41,976 --> 00:01:45,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">the derivatives of the output with respect to the input.</font>

24
00:01:45,532 --> 00:01:49,007
<font color="#FFFFFF">This representation has two attractive properties.</font>

25
00:01:49,087 --> 00:01:52,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, input gradients are the same size as the input,</font>

26
00:01:52,642 --> 00:01:56,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is far smaller than the enormous weight vector,</font>

27
00:01:56,198 --> 00:01:59,673
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they are much cheaper to compare with a kernel.</font>

28
00:01:59,753 --> 00:02:03,228
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

29
00:02:03,308 --> 00:02:06,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">forcing members to have different input gradients
means forcing them to depend on different input features.</font>

30
00:02:06,864 --> 00:02:10,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">Intuitively, this should make the ensemble more robust,</font>

31
00:02:10,419 --> 00:02:13,895
<font color="#FFFFFF">because if members react to complementary patterns,</font>

32
00:02:13,975 --> 00:02:17,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">corrupting one pattern will not fool all of them at once.</font>

33
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:21,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

34
00:02:21,996 --> 00:02:26,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces First-order Repulsive Deep Ensembles,</font>

35
00:02:26,161 --> 00:02:30,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">a method that adds a repulsion term defined on
input gradients rather than weights or function outputs.</font>

36
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:34,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

37
00:02:34,492 --> 00:02:38,578
<font color="#FFFFFF">it develops a practical kernel that compares the
normalized input gradients of the true label across training data,</font>

38
00:02:38,658 --> 00:02:42,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeping computation linear in the number of samples.</font>

39
00:02:42,823 --> 00:02:46,909
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

40
00:02:46,989 --> 00:02:51,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proposes a principled way to choose the kernel
lengthscales using the principal components of the data,</font>

41
00:02:51,154 --> 00:02:55,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">which lets FoRDE emphasize high-variance features
and become especially robust to input corruptions.</font>

42
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:59,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE is built on Wasserstein gradient descent,</font>

43
00:02:59,372 --> 00:03:03,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">a particle-based variational inference method.</font>

44
00:03:03,124 --> 00:03:06,796
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each network in the ensemble is a particle, and its
update has two terms.</font>

45
00:03:06,876 --> 00:03:10,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">The driving force pulls the particle toward
high-density regions of the Bayesian posterior,</font>

46
00:03:10,628 --> 00:03:14,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">just like ordinary training.</font>

47
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:18,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">The repulsion force, weighted by a kernel, pushes
particles apart.</font>

48
00:03:18,132 --> 00:03:21,804
<font color="#FFFFFF">The crucial design choice is the kernel.</font>

49
00:03:21,884 --> 00:03:25,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of comparing weights or outputs,</font>

50
00:03:25,636 --> 00:03:29,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE compares the input gradients of the true
label, normalized to the unit sphere,</font>

51
00:03:29,388 --> 00:03:33,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">using a radial basis function kernel.</font>

52
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:36,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gradients are normalized because their
magnitude shrinks as training converges,</font>

53
00:03:36,892 --> 00:03:40,564
<font color="#FFFFFF">and comparing directions teaches members to rely on
complementary features.</font>

54
00:03:40,644 --> 00:03:44,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally,</font>

55
00:03:44,396 --> 00:03:48,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">the lengthscales of the kernel are chosen from the
principal components of the data,</font>

56
00:03:48,148 --> 00:03:51,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">so repulsion is strongest along the most
informative input directions.</font>

57
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is tested across a broad range of settings.</font>

58
00:03:55,932 --> 00:04:01,726
<font color="#FFFFFF">Illustrative one-dimensional regression and
two-dimensional classification tasks show</font>

59
00:04:01,806 --> 00:04:06,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">how input-gradient repulsion increases uncertainty
away from the data.</font>

60
00:04:06,643 --> 00:04:12,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main image classification experiments use
CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and TinyImageNet,</font>

61
00:04:12,448 --> 00:04:17,206
<font color="#FFFFFF">with ResNet and PreActResNet backbones and an
ensemble of ten members.</font>

62
00:04:17,286 --> 00:04:22,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">To measure robustness, the authors use the
corrupted benchmarks CIFAR-10-C,</font>

63
00:04:22,469 --> 00:04:24,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-100-C, and TinyImageNet-C,</font>

64
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:29,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">which apply nineteen types of image corruption at
five severity levels.</font>

65
00:04:29,587 --> 00:04:34,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">They report accuracy, negative log-likelihood, and
expected calibration error,</font>

66
00:04:34,977 --> 00:04:38,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">both on clean data and averaged over all corruptions.</font>

67
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across the corrupted image benchmarks,</font>

68
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE with PCA lengthscales is the strongest method
on every metric,</font>

69
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:48,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">while remaining competitive on clean images.</font>

70
00:04:48,492 --> 00:04:50,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">Compared to the second-best method,</font>

71
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:58,027
<font color="#FFFFFF">it improves accuracy by about two point four
percent on CIFAR-10-C and about one point three percent on CIFAR-100-C.</font>

72
00:04:58,107 --> 00:05:02,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">The toy experiments tell a consistent story: in one
and two dimensions,</font>

73
00:05:02,628 --> 00:05:08,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE places higher uncertainty in regions away
from the training data than deep ensembles</font>

74
00:05:08,359 --> 00:05:10,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">and other repulsive methods,</font>

75
00:05:10,142 --> 00:05:15,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is direct evidence that repelling input
gradients yields greater functional diversity.</font>

76
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:19,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper carefully ablates the kernel lengthscales,</font>

77
00:05:19,647 --> 00:05:23,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">which govern how repulsion is distributed across
input dimensions.</font>

78
00:05:23,894 --> 00:05:27,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">The PCA lengthscales give the best robustness to
corruptions,</font>

79
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:30,701
<font color="#FFFFFF">because they emphasize high-variance features.</font>

80
00:05:30,781 --> 00:05:37,909
<font color="#FFFFFF">The identity lengthscales instead give the best
clean accuracy on CIFAR-100 and the best likelihood on CIFAR-10,</font>

81
00:05:37,989 --> 00:05:39,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">but sacrifice some robustness.</font>

82
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:44,796
<font color="#FFFFFF">Tuning between the two extremes yields the best of
both worlds in most cases.</font>

83
00:05:44,876 --> 00:05:50,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">A separate study varies the ensemble size and finds
that a FoRDE with ten members matches</font>

84
00:05:50,604 --> 00:05:55,415
<font color="#FFFFFF">or exceeds the corruption robustness of a deep
ensemble with thirty members,</font>

85
00:05:55,495 --> 00:05:58,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that the diversity gain is substantial.</font>

86
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:03,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

87
00:06:03,143 --> 00:06:07,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under input corruptions,</font>

88
00:06:07,466 --> 00:06:11,709
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE with PCA lengthscales improves accuracy by
about two point four percent on CIFAR-10-C</font>

89
00:06:11,789 --> 00:06:16,031
<font color="#FFFFFF">and one point three percent on CIFAR-100-C over the
next-best method.</font>

90
00:06:16,111 --> 00:06:20,354
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its diversity is efficient too: a ten-member FoRDE
reaches the same corruption robustness</font>

91
00:06:20,434 --> 00:06:24,677
<font color="#FFFFFF">as a thirty-member deep ensemble.</font>

92
00:06:24,757 --> 00:06:29,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">And these gains hold across a demanding benchmark
of nineteen corruption types at five severity levels each.</font>

93
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:35,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message of this paper is that the space
in which you enforce diversity matters as much as the amount.</font>

94
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:40,256
<font color="#FFFFFF">By repelling ensemble members in the compact space
of their input gradients,</font>

95
00:06:40,336 --> 00:06:46,226
<font color="#FFFFFF">FoRDE guarantees that members become genuinely
different functions that rely on complementary features,</font>

96
00:06:46,306 --> 00:06:51,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">without the waste of weight-space repulsion or the
intractability of function-space repulsion.</font>

97
00:06:51,755 --> 00:06:55,906
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combined with data-driven lengthscales from
principal component analysis,</font>

98
00:06:55,986 --> 00:07:01,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">this yields ensembles that are more robust and
better calibrated under the kinds of input</font>

99
00:07:01,145 --> 00:07:03,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">corruptions that matter in the real world.</font>
